{"create":"## What a task is\n\nA task describes the *how*: the implementation work needed to realize a\nfeature. Features describe the *what*. A task that drifts toward restating\nthe deliverable, embedding code, or recording decision rationale fails the\nreview pipeline — it becomes too long to read in one sitting.\n\n## Discipline\n\nCanonical writing standards live on @[feature:1a9ba140-7c7f-484d-8454-94f7d91f7a87|Task Tools].\nRead that feature for the full rule set: length budget, no embedded code,\nno decision archaeology, reference-don't-restate cross-cutting features,\nand signal-to-noise as the success metric.\n\n## Verify the implementation point before parenting\n\nWhen the task body names a symbol — a tool, file, endpoint, helper, type,\nevent, or similar — and you cannot point at the concrete implementation\nsite (the file, module, or feature subtree where it actually lives), run a\none-shot `unified_search` for that name before choosing the parent. The\ntrigger is \"I cannot name where this lives,\" not the kind of symbol.\n\nThe failure mode this prevents: parenting the task to the symptom-side\nfeature — the surface where the work is noticed — when the implementation\nlives somewhere else. Any examples are illustrative, not normative; the\nrule fires on the unverified-implementation-point condition, not on a\nclosed list of categories.\n\n## Tasks are ready to implement when written\n\nDo not author a task whose body defers a decision — a section telling\nthe implementer to decide, investigate, spike, or choose the approach\nbefore doing the work; settle it upstream (in a design doc, or before\n`create_work_item`) and write the task against the resolved answer — the\nforward-looking counterpart to the \"no decision archaeology\" rule.\n\n## Edges don't go in task content\n\nTyped cross-entity relationships go in the proposal's structured `edges`\nparameter — with the documented vocabulary (`RELATED_TO`, `DEPENDS_ON`, `DERIVES_FROM`, `SUPERSEDES`, `CONTRADICTS`) —\nnot narrated as prose inside the task body. The tool materializes real\ngraph edges on approval from that field; an edge written as text in the\ndescription is dead — invisible to `get_context`, not query-traversable,\nand untouched when the entities it names are renamed.\n\n`@[type:id|title]` mentions in prose remain encouraged as navigation, but\nthey are not a substitute for a typed edge. The failure mode this\nprevents: narrating \"this supersedes X\" in the description and assuming a\ngraph edge appears — it does not, because the `edges` field is the only\nsurface that materializes one.\n\n## Section shape\n\n**Description** — a paragraph or two of PM-audience prose that names\nwhat this task delivers and why. Reference the relevant features inline\nas `@[feature:id|name]` so the framing anchors back to the product\nsurface this work moves. No file paths, no class or function names,\nno architectural jargon — file-level context goes in the dedicated\n`implementation` field below, not in this prose.\n\n## The implementation field\n\n`create_work_item` accepts a dedicated `implementation` parameter,\nseparate from the section list. Fill it with your working knowledge of\nhow the work should be done — the relevant files, the patterns to\nfollow, the APIs to call, the architectural constraints to respect. The\ntool folds this into a trailing `## Implementation` block on the\ndescription, so do not hand-write that heading yourself and do not\nduplicate the detail in the description prose. Leave the field empty\nonly when there is genuinely no implementation context to offer.\n\n**Acceptance criteria** — a bulleted list of verifiable outcomes: one\n`- ` bullet per concrete, confirmable result, declarative present tense,\nno weasel words (\"should\", \"might\", \"could\"). Non-obvious scope\nboundaries may appear as additional bullets when they exist — omit\nthem when nothing genuinely non-obvious belongs.\n\n## Voice\n\nDeclarative present tense about the outcome. No weasel words (\"should\",\n\"might\", \"could\"). No process claims (\"we will\", \"the team will\").","edit":"## When to edit a task\n\nEdit when the user asks for a concrete change to a task's description,\nacceptance criteria, status, or other fields. Do not ask clarifying\nquestions when the user's intent is clear — make the edit.\n\n## Discipline\n\nThe discipline that governs task creation applies symmetrically here.\nCanonical writing standards live on @[feature:1a9ba140-7c7f-484d-8454-94f7d91f7a87|Task Tools] —\nedit paths cannot be a back door for rules a creation path enforces. An\nedit that pushes a task to 1500 words fails the same way a 1500-word\ncreation does.\n\n## Verify the implementation point before re-parenting\n\nEdits can move parenting too. When an edit changes the parent, or the\nedited body names a symbol — tool, file, endpoint, helper, type, event,\nor similar — whose concrete implementation site you cannot point at, run\na one-shot `unified_search` for that name before applying the edit. The\ntrigger is \"I cannot name where this lives,\" not the kind of symbol.\n\nThe failure mode this prevents: re-parenting the task to the symptom-side\nfeature — the surface where the work is noticed — when the implementation\nlives somewhere else. Any examples are illustrative, not normative; the\nrule fires on the unverified-implementation-point condition, not on a\nclosed list of categories.\n\n## Tasks are ready to implement when written\n\nAn edit must not introduce or preserve a section that defers a\ndecision — telling the implementer to decide, investigate, spike, or\nchoose the approach; settle it upstream (in a design doc, or before\nthe edit) and edit the task against the resolved answer — the\nforward-looking counterpart to the \"no decision archaeology\" rule.\n\n## Edges don't go in task content\n\nThe same rule governs edits — the edit path is otherwise a back door for\nit. Typed cross-entity relationships go in the proposal's structured\n`edges` parameter — with the documented vocabulary (`RELATED_TO`, `DEPENDS_ON`, `DERIVES_FROM`, `SUPERSEDES`, `CONTRADICTS`)\n— not added as prose to the task body. An edge written as text in the\ndescription is dead — invisible to `get_context`, not query-traversable,\nand untouched when the entities it names are renamed.\n\n`@[type:id|title]` mentions in prose remain encouraged as navigation, but\nthey are not a substitute for a typed edge. Narrating \"this supersedes X\"\nin the description does not make a graph edge appear — only the `edges`\nfield materializes one.\n\n## The implementation field\n\n`edit_work_item` accepts a dedicated `implementation` parameter,\nseparate from the section list. Fill it with your working knowledge of\nhow the work should be done — the relevant files, the patterns to\nfollow, the APIs to call, the architectural constraints to respect. The\ntool folds this into a trailing `## Implementation` block on the\ndescription, and appends to the existing description even when the edit\nsupplies no description section of its own. Do not hand-write that\nheading and do not duplicate the detail in the description prose. Leave\nthe field empty only when there is genuinely no implementation context\nto offer.\n\n## Common edit failure modes\n\n- **Description bloat.** Edits that pile on context until the task no\n  longer fits in one sitting. Trim hard.\n- **Decision archaeology creeping in.** \"We chose X because Y\" belongs\n  in a design doc, not a task. Propose `create_design_doc` if the user\n  wants the rationale captured.\n- **Acceptance criteria growing into a paragraph.** Keep each AC\n  bullet to one verifiable outcome — one sentence, declarative,\n  no weasel words.\n- **Cross-cutting feature references getting paraphrased.** Use\n  `@[feature:id|name]` and let the feature carry the contract.\n- **Deliverable framing buried under technical prose.** Edits that\n  push the PM-readable opening behind paragraphs of implementation\n  language. The first paragraph of a description must remain something\n  a non-technical PM can read; relocate technical detail into the\n  dedicated `implementation` field (it lands as a trailing\n  `## Implementation` block).\n- **File references scattered through the description.** Edits that\n  drop file paths inline wherever they happen to be relevant. Move\n  every file reference into the dedicated `implementation` field so\n  reviewers can find them in one place."}
