{"create":"## What a feature is\n\nA feature describes the *what*: the user-facing deliverable, scoped to\nits level of abstraction in the hierarchy. Tasks describe the *how*.\n\n## Discipline\n\nCanonical writing standards live on @[feature:39ee4ca3-d561-4442-9d24-884483cfc416|Feature Tools].\nRead that feature for the full rule set: scope-to-level-of-abstraction,\nreference-don't-restate, no embedded code, tag-as-routing-signal, and\nthe description-vs-requirements split.\n\n## Description — what is delivered\n\nDescribe the outcome that exists once the feature is complete. Length\nmatches the complexity of the feature: a broad parent may need several\nparagraphs; a leaf may need one.\n\nWhere they add clarity, supplement the prose with one of:\n- **User-facing features**: a user journey or ASCII interaction sketch.\n- **System/integration features**: a sequence diagram or system boundary diagram.\n- **Data or API features**: a schema sketch or request/response example.\n\nThese supplement the description; they do not replace it. Only include\nthem when prose alone would leave scope ambiguous.\n\nVoice: declarative present tense about the outcome. No architecture, no\nimplementation choices, no weasel words (\"should\", \"might\", \"could\"),\nno process claims (\"we will\", \"the team will\").\n\n## Requirements — what must be true\n\nRequirements are hard, declarative statements: *the system does X*, *the\nsystem rejects Y*, *every Z is visible to the owner*. They are concrete\nand inviolable — constraints, not guidelines. A requirement that can be\nsoftened, negotiated, or skipped is not a requirement.\n\nDistinct from description in three axes:\n- **Register** — declarative and terse, not narrative or explanatory.\n  Description says *why* and *what the experience feels like*;\n  requirements say *what must be true*.\n- **Structure** — a list of standalone, atomic statements. One\n  verifiable capability per item. Do not bundle multiple claims; do not\n  use prose.\n- **Voice** — present tense, active, subject-verb-object. \"The dashboard\n  shows the user's last login.\" Not \"users should be able to see...\"\n\nNo weasel words. No aspirational language. No implementation detail.\n\n## Tag honestly\n\nPick from `product`, `frontend`, `backend`, `infra`, `design`, `AI`.\nThe `AI` tag covers AI/agent-shaped delivery (prompts, agent\norchestration, runners) rather than ordinary backend code. The tag\nis load-bearing routing signal, not decoration. Top-level features\ndefault to `product`. A frontend slice that lives at the top level is\na structural smell — sub-features beneath the product concept they\nimplement is the right shape.","edit":"## When to edit a feature\n\nEdit when the user asks for a concrete change to a feature's\ndescription, requirements, tag, status, or other fields. Do not ask\nclarifying questions when the user's intent is clear.\n\n## Discipline\n\nThe discipline that governs feature creation applies symmetrically here.\nCanonical writing standards live on @[feature:39ee4ca3-d561-4442-9d24-884483cfc416|Feature Tools].\nEdit paths cannot be a back door for rules a creation path enforces.\n\n## Common edit failure modes\n\n- **Description and requirements get conflated.** When the user's\n  intent targets the wrong section (e.g. a declarative constraint\n  added to description), place it in the correct section instead.\n- **Requirements weakened or deleted silently.** Requirements are\n  inviolable. Sharpen wording or add missing ones; never weaken or\n  remove unless the user explicitly asks.\n- **Implementation detail leaks into description.** Architecture and\n  technical choices belong on tasks. Redirect.\n- **Tag-edit smells ignored.** If a frontend or backend slice is\n  sitting at the top level, surface the structural smell — propose a\n  re-parent rather than just adjusting the tag.\n\n## Roll-up check\n\nFeatures roll up: a parent's description implicitly covers everything\nin its children. After an edit, scan parent and child features in the\ntopology. If the change creates a scope conflict — a parent that no\nlonger covers a child, or a child that contradicts a parent — propose\nan edit on the related feature alongside the main edit, or\nflag the ambiguity to the user."}
$wit$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  true,
