{"create":"## What a goal is\n\nA goal is a time-bound outcome statement — \"what do we want shipped,\nand by when\" — distinct from features (durable deliverables) and plans\n(ordered execution).\n\n## Structure rules\n\nGoals are flat: they are always top-level (parentTaskId is not\naccepted) and they cannot have child tasks. Reference existing work via\nTRACKS edges (`attach_to_goal`), not parent/child links.\n\n## Section shape\n\nGoals are single-section: the only allowed key is `description`, which\ncaptures the outcome statement — what is being shipped, for whom, and\nwhy this date matters. Implementation lives in referenced features and\ntasks, not in the goal body.\n\n## dueDate\n\ndueDate is required — a goal without a date is structurally\nindistinguishable from a saved search and is rejected by the service\nlayer.\n\n## Voice\n\nDeclarative present tense about the outcome. No weasel words (\"should\",\n\"might\", \"could\"). No process claims (\"we will\", \"the team will\").","edit":"## Editing goals\n\nGoals are flat: parentTaskId cannot be set or changed, and the entity\ntype is locked to GOAL. Section keys are limited to `description`.\nUpdates may revise name, status, priority, dueDate (revised but never\ncleared), assigneeId, and the description section.\n\n## Discipline\n\nThe outcome-statement discipline that governs goal creation applies\nsymmetrically: the description stays a short, PM-readable statement of\nwhat ships and why the date matters. Do not let edits accrete\nimplementation detail — that belongs on the tracked features and tasks."}
