{"create":"## What a bug is\n\nA bug records a defect: observed behavior that contradicts the intended\nbehavior of a shipped surface. It is not a feature request and not a\nrefactor — work that changes intended behavior belongs on a STANDARD\nwork item or a feature.\n\n## Section shape\n\nBugs are single-section: `description` carries the report. Lead with the\nobserved behavior and the expected behavior in plain prose a PM can\nread. Include how to reproduce when known — the environment, the steps,\nand the entity or surface where the defect shows. Reference the affected\nfeatures inline as `@[feature:id|name]` rather than restating their\ncontracts.\n\n## Discipline\n\nThe task-writing discipline applies: short enough to read in one\nsitting, no embedded code dumps, no decision archaeology, no weasel\nwords. State what is broken and how that shows, not how to fix it —\nfix detail belongs on the implementing work item.\n\n## Severity and placement\n\nUse priority to carry severity. Parent the bug under the feature or\ntask that owns the broken behavior when the owner is known; a bug may\nstand top-level while the owning surface is being identified.","edit":"## Editing bugs\n\nEdit when the user asks for a concrete change to a bug's description,\nstatus, priority, or placement. The creation discipline applies\nsymmetrically: the description stays a short, PM-readable defect report\n— observed behavior, expected behavior, reproduction when known.\n\n## Common edit failure modes\n\n- **Fix detail creeping in.** How to fix the defect belongs on the\n  implementing work item, not the bug report.\n- **Report bloat.** Investigation notes piling up until the report no\n  longer reads in one sitting. Summarize; link the rest.\n- **Status drift.** A bug whose defect no longer reproduces is closed,\n  not rewritten into a different defect — open a new bug instead."}
